<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="73ac2534">&lt;p&gt;
    Understand how to create a Burndown Chart at both the sprint and release level in order to product an accurate view of
    the team&apos;s daily progress within a given sprint or release.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Create the Burndown Chart if one is not readily available to leverage. Use either an Agile tracking tool, such as JIRA
    or Agile Central, if available otherwise manually create one for the team.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    To create a manual version, sum up the total amount of story points, tasks or task hours for the work outlined in the
    Sprint Backlog. Plot this total effort estimate on the vertical axis (Y), using an appropriate scale. Next, plot all of
    the days allotted for the sprint on the horizontal axis (X).&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Use this graph to draw a line from the total sum of work, found at the top of the Y axis, to the last day of the
    sprint, found at the end of the X axis. This straight line represents the ideal burndown, or the speed at which the
    team would complete work, if working at a uniform speed throughout the entire sprint.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Once a Burndown Chart has been created and made available, remember to update it on a regular basis. The manner in
    which the chart is updated will depend on the type or format being used. For manual Burndown Charts, update it during
    daily Scrum meetings to reflect all work completed since the last meeting. Burndown Charts being maintained by an Agile
    tracking tool will dynamically update to show progress as work items are marked complete, or task hour estimates are
    updated, by team members.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Update the manual Burndown Chart to reflect the latest sum of story points, tasks or task hours for all remaining, or
    incomplete, work in the Sprint Backlog on a given day. To do this, plot those remaining story points, tasks or task
    hours for all outstanding work items against the specific day in the sprint to see the actual pace at which the team is
    delivering. If the team is delivering work at the pace originally estimated and expected, the actual burndown line will
    follow the ideal burndown line, plotted at the beginning of the sprint before any work commenced. The chart should
    always reflect a downward trend unless one of the following exceptions has occurred:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Work added mid-sprint
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        New tasks identified, after sprint planning, as being necessary to complete a Backlog Item
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Remaining task estimate re-assessed to be higher than originally anticipated during sprint planning
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    However, if the actual burndown value plotted appears above the ideal burndown line, this indicates the team is behind
    schedule. Alternatively, the team is working ahead of schedule when the actual burndown value plotted appears below the
    one for ideal burndown.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It&amp;nbsp;is also possible to&amp;nbsp;create a Burndown Chart for an entire release, if needed, by reflecting all of the
    work to be conducted across all sprints as opposed to a subset selected for a single sprint as explained above. To do
    this,&amp;nbsp;follow the same guidance above but plot all planned sprints on the X axis, instead of&amp;nbsp;days, and plot
    story points on&amp;nbsp;the Y axis.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
